//@version=6
indicator("UCTPROBE_GB_BARSSINCE_2ARG", overlay = false)

// ⭐⭐⭐ THE ONE PROBE WHOSE EXPECTED RESULT IS A COMPILE FAILURE, AND THAT IS
// WHY IT IS ALONE IN A FILE.
//
// `closedTable.json` declares `barssince(series, int)`. All 12 corpus call sites
// pass ONE argument, and TradingView documents `ta.barssince(condition)` — one
// argument. `docs/pine/r11-group-b-arity.md` states the stakes precisely: "if the
// table is wrong, 'widening to 1' would be NARROWING TO THE TRUTH, and the 2-arg
// form we currently accept is a form the vendor may reject outright. That has to
// be read off the vendor before either arity is touched, because THE TWO POSSIBLE
// FIXES ARE OPPOSITE."
//
// ⛔⛔ SO THE TWO ARITIES CANNOT SHARE A SCRIPT. A Pine script is compiled as a
// whole: if the 2-arg form is invalid, a file containing BOTH forms produces no
// study at all and the 1-arg reading is lost with it. Worse, the failed study
// still lands on the chart wearing a one-plot stub whose roster reads fine
// (measured 2026-09-10). So the 1-arg form lives in
// `groupb-barssince-1arg.pine` and this file holds only the form under suspicion.
//
// ⭐⭐ HOW TO READ THIS PROBE — AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF EVERY OTHER ONE HERE:
//
//   COMPILE FAILS  ⇒ the 2-arg form is NOT valid Pine ⇒ OUR TABLE IS WRONG.
//                    The fix is to NARROW `barssince` to one argument. Record the
//                    vendor's error code, message, line and column from
//                    `status().errorDescription.editorError` — that text IS the
//                    measurement, and `isFailed()` is how you know to read it.
//
//   COMPILE SUCCEEDS ⇒ the 2-arg form IS valid ⇒ the table is right and the
//                    corpus simply never uses it. Then N02/N03 below say what the
//                    second argument DOES, which is the follow-on question.
//
// ⛔ A GREEN RUN IS NOT A PASS HERE. "It compiled" is one of two answers, not
// success, and recording it as success would flip the ruling to the wrong fix.
// The pairing with the 1-arg probe is what makes either answer meaningful: if
// BOTH fail, the problem is this harness, not the arity.

// ── the control ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
plot(bar_index, "N01_bar_index_CONTROL")                                   // N01

// ── the form under suspicion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A condition that is true often enough to keep the result small and readable.
cond = close > open
plot(ta.barssince(cond, 10), "N02_barssince_2arg_RAW")                     // N02

// ⭐ IF IT COMPILES, THIS SAYS WHETHER THE SECOND ARGUMENT CHANGES ANYTHING.
// 0 would mean the extra argument is inert on this data — which would be its own
// finding, and a different one from "the argument is a lookback cap".
plot(ta.barssince(cond, 10) - ta.barssince(cond, 500),
     "N03_2arg_10_minus_500_0_means_INERT_here")                           // N03
